2.3. Functional Requirements 
2.3. Functional Requirements

The following functional requirements define the main functions of the AR-IMMS (Augmented Reality Infrastructure Monitoring and Maintenance System). Each requirement is assigned a unique identifier (FR01–FR18) to support requirement traceability, Use Case Specifications, and the Requirement Traceability Matrix (RTM).

2.3.1. Authentication

FR01 – Login
The system shall allow Administrators, Operators, and Technicians to log in using valid credentials.

FR02 – Logout
The system shall allow authenticated users to securely log out of the system.

FR03 – Password Change
The system shall allow authenticated users to change their passwords.

2.3.2. User and Role Management

FR04 – Manage User Accounts
The system shall allow the Administrator to create, view, update, and delete user accounts.

FR05 – Manage User Roles and Permissions
The system shall allow the Administrator to assign and manage roles and permissions for Administrators, Operators, and Technicians.

2.3.3. Infrastructure Management

FR06 – Manage Infrastructure
The system shall allow the Administrator to manage the infrastructure hierarchy consisting of Site, Room, Rack, Server, and Container.

FR07 – View Infrastructure Information
The system shall allow Administrators, Operators, and Technicians to view infrastructure information and the current status of monitored components.

2.3.4. Telemetry Management

FR08 – Collect and Send Telemetry
The system shall allow the Collector to collect real-time CPU, RAM, Disk, and Network metrics from monitored infrastructure components and send the collected telemetry data to the Backend.

FR09 – Store and View Telemetry
The system shall store received telemetry data and allow authorized users to view historical and current telemetry information.

2.3.5. Alert Management

FR10 – Detect Anomalies
The system shall automatically detect abnormal infrastructure conditions based on predefined thresholds.

FR11 – Manage Alert Lifecycle
The system shall allow authorized users to view and manage alerts through the following lifecycle:

OPEN → ACKNOWLEDGED → RESOLVED → CLOSED

2.3.6. Incident Management

FR12 – Manage Incident
The system shall allow authorized users to create and manage incidents when an alert requires formal investigation or handling.

2.3.7. Ticket Management

FR13 – Create and Assign Ticket
The system shall allow authorized users to create maintenance tickets and assign them to Technicians.

FR14 – Manage Ticket Status
The system shall allow authorized users to update ticket status through the following lifecycle:

OPEN → IN_PROGRESS → RESOLVED → CLOSED

2.3.8. Maintenance Management

FR15 – Record Maintenance
The system shall allow Technicians to record maintenance results, including maintenance description, time, and technician information.

2.3.9. Digital Twin

FR16 – Synchronize Digital Twin
The system shall synchronize the current status of physical infrastructure devices with their corresponding digital representations based on updated infrastructure and telemetry data.

2.3.10. Augmented Reality

FR17 – Identify Device Using AR
The system shall allow Technicians to scan QR codes or ArUco markers to identify physical infrastructure devices.

FR18 – View Device Information Using AR
The system shall allow Technicians to view device information, telemetry, alerts, and tickets through the Mobile AR interface.
2.4. Non-functional Requirements
The non-functional requirements of the AR-IMMS system are specified in detail based on 7 core technical criteria:
- Performance:
+ The default telemetry data collection frequency from servers is set to 5 seconds per time.
+ Ensure query speeds and historical telemetry data chart rendering do not disrupt user experience.
- Security:
+ Authenticate users and secure API requests via JWT authentication tokens.
+ Granular resource access authorization according to the RBAC model for 3 role groups: Administrator, Operator, and Technician.
+ Transmission encryption using TLS/mTLS protocols for connections between Collector Agent and Backend.
+ Automatic audit logging for data modification or system assignment operations.
- Availability:
+ Automatically evaluate and update server status to Offline/Unavailable if telemetry data reception stops for over 90 seconds.
+ Ensure the Backend system and Database maintain continuous operational status for infrastructure monitoring tasks.
- Scalability:
+ Architectural design allowing flexible expansion of connecting additional target servers (Collector Agents) without altering the core structure.
- Real-time:
+ Use the Socket.IO protocol to transmit changing telemetry data and alert statuses to Web Dashboard and Mobile AR instantly without page refreshes.
- Reliability:
+ Collector Agent features built-in Retry mechanisms upon data sending failure and automatic Reconnect upon transmission connection loss.
+ The alert processing engine features Duplicate Suppression to avoid generating multiple identical Alerts for the same incident.
- Usability:
+ The Web Dashboard interface is designed according to responsive standards, supporting intuitive hierarchical Digital Twin model monitoring.
+ The Android-based Mobile AR application is optimized for QR/ArUco Marker scanning operations and smooth AR information overlay display on-site.
2.5. Business Rules
Business rules define the logical constraints, trigger conditions, and automated operational workflows throughout the AR-IMMS system:
- Closed-loop Incident Resolution Workflow (Incident Lifecycle): The operational incident processing sequence must strictly follow the transition flow: Telemetry (threshold exceeded) → Alert → Incident → Ticket → Technician → Maintenance → Resolution.
- Alert Detection & Duplicate Suppression: Alerts are automatically generated when Telemetry metrics exceed configured thresholds (e.g., CPU > 80%, RAM > 90%, Disk > 90%). For continuous incidents occurring on the same device and metric, the system updates the existing alert instead of creating duplicate alerts. 
- State Machine Rules: The statuses of Alerts and Tickets must be strictly managed according to their lifecycle:
+ Alert Lifecycle: OPEN → ACKNOWLEDGE → RESOLVED → CLOSED.
+ Ticket Lifecycle: OPEN → IN_PROGRESS → RESOLVED → CLOSED.
- Offline Detection Rule: When the system receives no Telemetry data from a server's Collector Agent for more than 90 seconds (exceeding 18 5-second data transmission cycles), that server's status is automatically updated to Unavailable/Offline and triggers the corresponding alert.
- Access Control & Authorization (RBAC & Authorization Rules):
+ Technician: Only has access rights to perform AR code scanning operations and handle directly assigned Tickets.
+ Operator: Has full authority to monitor the entire infrastructure, receive Alerts, create Incidents, as well as create and assign Tickets to Technicians.
+ Administrator: Holds full privileges to manage users, roles, system configurations, and retrieve Audit Logs.
- Maintenance Acceptance & Ticket Closing (Maintenance & Closing Rule): A Ticket only transitions to the RESOLVED status after the Technician conducts on-device inspection via Mobile AR and completes the Maintenance Record. The incident ticket is only officially changed to CLOSED upon confirmation and approval from the Operator.
2.6. Use Case Diagram

The Use Case Diagram provides an overall view of the interactions between the main actors and the AR-IMMS system. It identifies the major functions provided by the system and describes how each actor interacts with these functions.

The AR-IMMS system has four main actors: Administrator, Operator, Technician, and Collector.

The Administrator is responsible for managing user accounts, roles and permissions, infrastructure information, and system activities. The Administrator can also monitor infrastructure and manage alerts, incidents, and tickets according to assigned permissions.

The Operator is responsible for monitoring infrastructure, viewing telemetry data, and handling alerts, incidents, and tickets during daily infrastructure operations.

The Technician is responsible for monitoring infrastructure, reviewing telemetry data, handling alerts and incidents, processing maintenance tickets, performing maintenance activities, and using Mobile AR to identify devices and view device information.

The Collector is an external agent responsible for collecting infrastructure metrics from monitored devices and transmitting telemetry data to the Backend.

The main use cases of AR-IMMS are derived from the functional requirements defined in Section 2.3.

2.6.1. Overall Use Case Diagram

The Overall Use Case Diagram illustrates the main interactions between the four actors and the AR-IMMS system.

The Administrator interacts with authentication, user and role management, infrastructure management, monitoring, alert, incident, and ticket management functions.

The Operator interacts with authentication, infrastructure monitoring, telemetry visualization, alert management, incident management, and ticket management functions.

The Technician interacts with authentication, infrastructure monitoring, telemetry visualization, alert and incident management, ticket processing, maintenance, and AR-based functions.

The Collector interacts with the system by collecting infrastructure metrics and sending telemetry data to the Backend.

The system automatically processes telemetry data to detect abnormal conditions and update the corresponding Digital Twin representation of infrastructure devices.

